## 2026-03-19: Styling stack — BaseUI + CSS Modules
Tailwind CSS and shadcn/ui are not used. The UI stack is Base UI (unstyled accessible primitives) + CSS Modules for all styling. CSS Module files are named `ComponentName.module.css` and co-located with their component. Inline style objects are only acceptable for dynamic computed values.

## 2026-03-19: DES:: epic prefix for Design work
Added `DES::` as a new Jira epic team prefix for Design epics (mockups, UX review, design system work). Distinct from `DE::` which means Desktop/Tauri/Rust. The full epic prefix set is now: BE, FE, DE, DES, INF.

## 2026-03-19: .cursor/rules/ as the single convention source
`.cursor/rules/*.mdc` is the single source of truth for all conventions — shared by both Cursor and Claude Code. `.claude/CLAUDE.md` is a pointer file that directs Claude Code to read the `.cursor/rules/` files. No conventions are duplicated across the two locations.

## 2026-05-04: Full stack definition — CF-native, TanStack Start, server-authoritative offline

**Stack — locked:**

| Layer | Choice |
|---|---|
| Web framework | TanStack Start (SSR for web, SPA mode for desktop) |
| Desktop shell | Tauri 2.0 — wraps TanStack Start SPA build. No separate React app in apps/desktop/. |
| Mobile | Expo / React Native (future) |
| API | Hono on Cloudflare Workers — same Worker as TanStack Start, mounted at /api/$ |
| Database | Neon (Postgres) |
| ORM | Drizzle + drizzle-zod |
| Connection pooling | Cloudflare Hyperdrive |
| Auth | Better Auth |
| Tenant isolation | Postgres RLS (set via session context in transaction wrapper) |
| Client state | TanStack Query — structured to migrate to TanStack DB when v1 ships |
| Offline | Server-authoritative + optimistic updates + durable SQLite mutation queue |
| Offline SQLite | wa-sqlite/OPFS (web), native Tauri SQL plugin (desktop), expo-sqlite (mobile) |
| Source sync | Cloudflare Queues (webhook ingest) + CF Cron Triggers (Jira polling fallback) |
| Real-time | Deferred post-v1 — CF Durable Objects + WebSocket |
| Type flow | Drizzle schema → drizzle-zod → Hono RPC → hc typed client → component props |

**Build targets — one TanStack Start codebase, two outputs:**
- `BUILD_TARGET=web` → SSR output, deploys to CF Workers
- `BUILD_TARGET=desktop` → SPA mode (`spa: { enabled: true }`), outputs static `dist/`, Tauri packages it
- Server functions in SPA mode automatically become HTTP calls to the Hono API — no code changes required

**Tauri architecture:**
- `apps/desktop/` is Rust shell only — Tauri config, native plugins, no React
- Main window loads TanStack Start SPA at `/`
- Tray window is a second Tauri window loading the same SPA at `/tray` — one binary, two windows
- Both windows share one native SQLite database via Tauri SQL plugin
- Main window owns the mutation queue drainer; tray window is a reader/writer only
- Hono API must allow `tauri://localhost` as a CORS origin

**Monorepo structure:**
```
apps/
  web/          TanStack Start (SSR for web, SPA for Tauri)
  desktop/      Tauri shell only (Rust, tauri.conf.json, native plugins)
  mobile/       Expo (future)
  api/          Hono on CF Workers
packages/
  ui/           Shared React components
  api-client/   Typed Hono hc client — used by web, desktop, mobile
  queue/        Mutation queue abstraction — pure TS, platform-agnostic drivers
  db/           Drizzle schema + drizzle-zod types — imported by api/ only
```

**Offline mutation queue:**
1. User action → serializable command with client UUID (idempotency key)
2. Optimistic update applied to TanStack Query cache immediately
3. Command persisted to SQLite queue (survives app kills)
4. Background drainer sends commands to Hono API FIFO with exponential backoff
5. Server applies RLS + business rules + optimistic concurrency, returns canonical state
6. Server idempotency ledger enables safe retries (the early `processed_mutations` table was
   deferred from MVP on 2026-06-04)
7. Conflicts: 409 version / 422 business rule / 403 permission → rollback + rejected-mutation UI

**Source integration:**
- Sync boundary: Jira Board (within Space), Linear Team, GitHub Repository
- Status mapping defined at connection time — display always uses source vocabulary
- Webhook ingest → CF Queue → consumer Worker → Neon
- CF Cron Trigger polls Jira periodically (Jira webhooks are best-effort, no replay)

**Core data model:**
```
Spec (synced from source)
  └── Action (Stride-native, 1+ per Spec)
        └── Session (Stride-native, time block on an Action)

Standalone Action (no parent Spec — personal tasks, title + estimate only)
  └── Session
```
Execution always ties to an Action. Sessions never attach directly to a Spec.

**Explicitly rejected — do not reintroduce without a new decision entry:**
- PowerSync (RLS gap on client-side SQLite)
- Supabase (ecosystem lock-in)
- TanStack DB in production now (persistence alpha as of 0.6)
- ElectricSQL as primary sync layer
- Separate TanStack Start app for Tauri (one codebase, two build outputs)
- Tray as a separate Tauri binary (one binary, two windows)
- Stride-native Specs (all Specs have a source system; standalone tasks use Actions)
- Sessions tied directly to Specs (must go through an Action)

## 2026-05-12: Desktop integration — refinements to the 2026-05-04 stack

Builds on the 2026-05-04 entry; fills in specifics that were left implicit. None contradict it. Some points are still to be confirmed when `apps/desktop` is built (tracked as Q18–Q20 in `docs/product/open-questions.md`).

- **Data boundary is `packages/api-client` (typed Hono `hc`), not TanStack Start server functions.** Its base URL is an env var — `/api/$` on the same Worker for the web/SSR build, the remote API origin for the desktop SPA build — so the data layer is byte-identical across builds. Server functions (`createServerFn`) are reserved for genuine server-only context (request headers/cookies, secrets). Supersedes the loose "server functions in SPA mode automatically become HTTP calls" framing above.
- **On desktop, the mutation-queue drainer runs in the Tauri Rust backend**, not a webview — single owner, survives window close; both webviews enqueue commands via Tauri commands. Replaces "main window owns the drainer."
- **Desktop auth transport: a bearer token in the OS keychain** (Better Auth bearer mode for desktop, cookie mode for web). Avoids cross-site-cookie friction with the `tauri://` webview origin.
- **The TanStack Query cache is persisted** (`@tanstack/query-persist-client` — OPFS on web, a file on desktop) so a cold start while offline shows last-fetched data, with the optimistic outbox layered over it.

To confirm when `apps/desktop` exists: bearer-vs-cookie (leaning bearer), desktop OAuth-callback handling (`tauri-plugin-deep-link` vs localhost loopback), final confirmation of the Rust-side drainer.

## 2026-05-21: Privacy-as-data-integrity, and architectural commitments behind the v1 re-cut

The v1 *scope* re-cut (thin execution loop; see `docs/product/mvp.md`) is a product
decision recorded there — not in this log. What is recorded here are its **architectural
consequences**, which are decisions that constrain the schema and API from the first line
of code and would be expensive or impossible to retrofit.

Context: Stride's value depends on honest user data, and a person only keeps their data
honest if they trust it won't be used against them (trust → honest input → accurate data →
product value). So privacy is a *data-integrity requirement*, not a values statement. Full
reasoning and the enforced commitments are in `docs/product/principles.md`.

Locked architectural commitments — honor them from the first endpoint/table:

- **Asymmetric access.** Per-person signal lives in the DB, but the API surface for
  aggregates **cannot return individual rows** — individual decomposition is not
  expressible, not merely filtered out after the fact.
- **Honest deletion.** User-data deletion is a **hard delete** that triggers aggregate
  recomputation without the deleted data — distinct from the operational soft-delete
  (`deleted` flag) used for sync/offline correctness in `db-patterns.mdc`.
- **Source-native storage.** Source-specific fields (Jira Sprint, Linear Cycle, GitHub
  Milestone, custom fields) are stored source-native; normalization happens at the
  display layer. Stride has no unified Project/Sprint entity. (Reinforces the existing
  "no Stride-native Specs" rule.)
- **Cross-cutting attachment point.** A single empty junction table ships in v1 so a
  future "Concept" entity can attach to any Stride entity. Not populated, not surfaced.
- **API as product.** The internal API is designed as if external systems will consume it
  (typed responses, stable paths, documented schemas, permission scopes). MCP server,
  webhooks, REST endpoints, SQL views are all *future adapters* on this internal API.
- **Provenance-ready.** When the first derived value ships (post-v1, with Insights) it
  carries lineage metadata from day one. Not built in v1, but not designed out of.
- **Privacy defaults** favour the individual — presence / focus-status indicators default
  **off** in the schema, opt-in only.

Does not contradict the 2026-05-04 or 2026-05-12 entries; the locked stack stands.

## 2026-06-02: App shell is web-owned, shared UI has no templates layer

The sidebar/app shell carries TanStack Router, workspace/account selection, profile status,
settings navigation, and platform-specific tray-preview behavior. That makes it app
composition, not shared UI. `AppShell` lives in `apps/web/src/components/AppShell` and is
imported by the web providers. `packages/ui` no longer exposes a `templates` layer; shared
UI is atoms, molecules, and organisms only. Modal and Drawer remain shared molecules because
they compose primitives into reusable accessibility patterns without app routing or data
assumptions.

## 2026-06-02: Backend platform confirmed — Cloudflare + Neon (Convex declined); v1 schema-shaping resolutions

The 2026-05-25 reconsideration (`docs/architecture/backend-platform-considerations.md`) is
**closed in favour of the already-locked Cloudflare + Neon stack** (2026-05-04). Convex was
weighed as a reactive-DB alternative and **declined**: it trades away Postgres portability
and the "API as product" surface (2026-05-21) for built-in realtime that v1 does not need
(realtime stays deferred to Durable Objects post-v1). Convex remains only a documented
benchmark / escape hatch, not a path. The locked stack stands unchanged.

Resolved at the same time (product/schema decisions; full detail lives in
`docs/product/data-model.md` and `docs/product/open-questions.md`, summarised here because
they constrain the first tables):

- **Backlog state is derived, not stored (Q4).** The "Later / Snoozed / Archive / Reassign"
  framing was stale prototype-era text and is dropped — none of it exists in the built
  backlog. The view tabs (Breakdown / In Flight / Blocked / Next Up / Completed) and a Spec's
  `readiness` are **computed** from primitives (action count, estimates set, `done`,
  blocked-ness, assignee), never stored as columns, so they cannot drift. The schema stores
  the primitives plus the internal `Spec.status` (`open | closed`) already in the data model.
- **Reopen is a manual status flip (Q8).** Reopening a closed Spec flips `Spec.status` back
  to `open`; Actions, Sessions, and past ScheduledEvents are left untouched as immutable
  history. No automatic revival, no new-cycle entity.
- **Session keeps a content-signal slot from day one (Q21).** v1 records timeshape only, but
  the Session table ships a nullable `contentSignal` (JSON) + `signalSource` column so git /
  file correlation can land later with no migration. Honours the provenance-ready commitment
  (2026-05-21) without pulling desktop/git work into the MVP.
- **Onboarding is account-first (Q11).** A person creates a User account first, then runs
  workspace creation (plan preview → name → invite teammates by email). The founder flow
  mints User + Workspace + workspace Membership(`admin`); invite acceptance is a magic link
  that mints a workspace Membership(`member`) and drops them inside the workspace. There is
  no standalone signup surface; `/auth/login` serves returning users. Billing stays a preview
  (deferred).

Does not contradict the 2026-05-04 / 2026-05-12 / 2026-05-21 entries; it confirms and
extends them.

## 2026-06-02: `@stride/db` schema scoped from the built frontend, not just the plan doc

The first schema pass (10 tables) was derived from `data-model.md`. A feature scan of the
built `apps/web` surfaces showed several production-grade features the plan under-specified, so
the schema was expanded to 17 tables to support what actually exists. Decisions:

- **The team layer ships in the schema now** (`teams`, `teamMembers`, `teamSourceMappings`,
  `specs.teamId`). The FE builds on Team heavily and the source-sync boundary is per-Team.
  Consistent with the 2026-05-21 cut ("team layer — RLS/foundations stay; only UI deferred"):
  the *data foundations* land; the team *UI/flows* remain deferred. Source-unit→team binding
  and status/priority/difficulty mappings live on `teamSourceMappings`, not on the pooled
  `sourceConnections` (workflows differ per board/team/repo).
- **The Schedule plan layer is a real table** (`scheduledEvents`). It is distinct from the
  actual layer (`sessions`). Superseded 2026-06-03/04: schedule event types are workspace rows
  seeded with defaults, and recurrence stores RRULE + timezone + exceptions/overrides.
- **Three cross-cutting surfaces get lean tables**: `notifications` (the Inbox — single-player
  gets `assigned`/`source_drift`, team types populate later), `specLinks` (source-synced
  Blocks/Blocked-by/Related/Implements), and `specActivity` (append-only, non-derivable audit
  events — backs the ownership/provenance commitment; no soft delete, no `updatedAt`).
  **Presence/profile-statuses was deliberately NOT modelled** (deferred; `memberships.presenceEnabled`
  default-false stands as the privacy anchor).
- **Derive, don't store** (kept out of the schema on purpose): `readiness`, the backlog view
  tabs, the `attention` flags (`unassigned` / `closed-in-source` / `just-landed` — the last from
  `specs.assignedAt`), `action.scheduled` and future-scheduled minutes, `recommendationReason`,
  a Spec's display `type`, and the reconstructable parts of the Spec history timeline.
- **Settings are jsonb, not columns** (`memberships.notificationPrefs`, `users.settings`) —
  config, not relational data. The original `workspaces.settings` and `teams.settings`
  placeholders are superseded by the 2026-06-03 settings decisions below.
- Added `actions.difficulty` as a real stored field. `captures.kind` was later deferred with
  the capture table in the 2026-06-04 MVP cleanup.

Deferred (not in the schema): calendar-connection tables, billing, the person-level
blocker/nudge chokepoint graph (`waitingOn`/`blocking`), and capture/concept learning tables.

## 2026-06-02: Deployment environments — three isolated rungs, promote-the-same-commit, migrate-on-deploy

Three persistent environments, no UAT yet, designed so deploying is boring and the unsafe
action is the hard one. Full operational detail in `docs/architecture/environments.md`.

- **dev → staging → production.** dev auto-deploys on merge to `main` (integration + QA +
  dogfooding); staging and production are one-click promotions, production gated by a required
  second approver (GitHub Environments protection rules). **UAT is deliberately not built** —
  added between dev and staging only when a non-developer needs a stable sign-off env (first
  external beta), otherwise it's an empty gate.
- **Databases fully isolated; no prod data downstream.** `stride-prod` is a separate Neon
  project from `stride-nonprod` (dev + staging branches). **No environment is a copy-on-write
  branch of production** — staging gets prod-*shaped* data from the seed generator, never prod
  rows. COW is used only off a *seed* "golden" branch for ephemeral E2E databases. This honours
  the privacy/data-ownership principles (`docs/product/principles.md`): real user data never
  sits in a testing environment.
- **Promote the same commit.** Build/test runs once on a SHA; staging and prod deploy that
  identical SHA — what was smoke-tested in staging is byte-for-byte what reaches prod. Rollback
  is just promoting an older commit. No per-env rebuild.
- **Migrations always ride with the deploy, every env.** `pnpm db:generate` produces committed
  SQL; each deploy runs `pnpm db:migrate` against that env's **direct** Neon URL (never through
  Hyperdrive) *before* deploying code. Migrate gates the deploy and is idempotent, so nothing is
  ever half-applied and any deploy is safe to re-run. Two CI guards keep this safe with no
  operator knowledge required: a **schema-drift check** (fails if the schema changed without a
  committed migration) and a **destructive-migration guard** (fails on DROP/type-change/NOT-NULL
  additions unless the file carries a `-- @safety:reviewed <reason>` marker).
- **Expand/contract is the rule** that makes auto-migrate-on-deploy safe: a migration must never
  break the currently-running version (Workers roll out gradually; offline desktop clients replay
  a queued mutation stream). Additive changes ship with their code; removals/renames/type-changes
  split across two releases.
- **Hotfixes** branch off the production release tag (not `main`), validate on a disposable
  ephemeral Neon-branch + preview-Worker in CI (staging's release candidate untouched), promote
  to prod, then merge back to `main`.
- **One seed definition** (`packages/db/src/seed.ts`, deterministic, deliberately adversarial)
  feeds local dev, staging, and the E2E golden branch. `pnpm db:reset` (drop→migrate→seed) is
  guarded to local-only. **Feature flags** stay a separate runtime system keyed off a non-secret
  `STRIDE_ENV` var, gating server-side, never inferred from the DB URL.

Does not contradict the 2026-05-04 / 2026-05-12 / 2026-05-21 / earlier 2026-06-02 entries; it
operationalises the locked Cloudflare + Neon stack.

## 2026-06-02: CI tooling — Depot runners + Infisical secrets (no .env, no GitHub secret store)

Refines the deployment-environments entry (same day) with the build/secret tooling. Operational
detail in `docs/architecture/environments.md` (Tooling section).

- **Depot for CI runners.** Drop-in replacement for GitHub-hosted runners (`runs-on:
  depot-ubuntu-24.04`), ~3× faster with an automatic distributed cache (turbo/pnpm/Docker).
  **GitHub Actions remains the orchestrator** — Depot is runners + cache, not a workflow engine.
  No YAML restructuring; only `runs-on` changes.
- **Infisical is the secrets source of truth — not `.env` files, not GitHub Secrets.** Per
  environment (`dev`/`staging`/`production`, plus a `ci` env for Neon API creds). Local dev uses
  `infisical run -- <cmd>` after `infisical login`; CI uses a **keyless OIDC machine identity**
  (`Infisical/secrets-action`) so **no long-lived secrets live in GitHub** (only non-secret
  `identity-id`/`project-slug` as GitHub variables). Worker runtime secrets reach Cloudflare via
  Infisical's native Cloudflare Workers secret sync — no manual `wrangler secret put`.
- **GitHub Environments are kept strictly for approval gates** (required reviewer on production),
  not secret storage — a clean split from Infisical.

Does not contradict earlier entries; it operationalises the 2026-06-02 environments decision.

## 2026-06-03: Schedule types are workspace preferences; external calendar events stay source-owned

Supersedes the Schedule-plan-layer bullet in the 2026-06-02 schema entry.

- **Scheduled event types are rows, not a fixed enum.** `scheduled_event_types` is
  workspace-scoped and seeded with defaults. Users can add, rename, recolor, reorder, and archive
  non-system types. `scheduled_events.typeId` points at that table so historical events retain
  their category for reporting.
- **System schedule types preserve app-owned behavior.** `systemKey` marks required types such as
  `actions` and `external_calendar`. These remain available even if the workspace customizes the
  rest of its schedule taxonomy.
- **External calendar events are immutable imported facts.** Stride stores them with the
  `external_calendar` system type, source provenance, free/busy availability, and opaque
  `externalMetadata`. There is no local `externalClassification`; renaming or recategorizing the
  source event happens outside Stride.

Also supersedes the Neon names in the 2026-06-02 deployment entry: the actual projects are
`Stride Production` and `Stride Development`, and ephemeral E2E branches copy from `baseline`
(not `golden`).

## 2026-06-03: Spec status comes from source status mappings, not a local open/closed enum

Supersedes the `specs.status` part of the 2026-06-02 schema entry.

- **Specs do not have a Stride-native `open | closed` lifecycle enum.** Source systems own the
  status vocabulary and status changes happen through the mapped source status picker.
- `specs.sourceStatus` stores the current source-native status label/key for display.
- `specs.mappedStatus` stores the stable Team mapping key used for filtering and derived views.
- `teamSourceMappings.statusMapping` owns source-status → mapped-status metadata because workflows
  differ by board/team/repo. The pooled source connection does not own status semantics.

## 2026-06-03: Source mappings own badge metadata for status, priority, and difficulty

Clarifies the 2026-06-02 schema entry and the source-mapping settings surface.

- Team Source mapping has three mapped sections: status, priority, and difficulty.
- Each section maps a source key/label to a Stride mapped badge definition: stable mapped key,
  display mode (`text` or `icon`), optional text/icon value, and color.
- Specs store `sourceStatus`/`mappedStatus` and `sourcePriority`/`mappedPriority` separately.
- Specs also store `sourceDifficulty`/`mappedDifficulty`. Actions keep a Stride-owned
  `difficulty`; they are not source-mapped.

## 2026-06-04: MVP schema cleanup after source/schema review

- Cut `captures`, `conceptLinks`, and `processedMutations` from MVP. Capture/learning and the
  offline mutation queue return when their full behaviors are designed.
- Sessions no longer embed `notes`, `jots`, or `endNote`; `session_notes` is the crawlable note
  timeline for manual notes, session-end feedback, and future capture-originated notes.
- Actions keep a Stride-owned `difficulty`; Specs carry source/mapped difficulty alongside
  source/mapped status and priority.
- Scheduled recurrence uses RRULE + timezone + exceptions/overrides. External event editability
  is derived from external provenance rather than a separate `fixed` flag.
- Schedule type colors/icons and user accent colors use curated token keys backed by hex/icon
  maps, so palette/icon changes can happen centrally without breaking stored rows.

## 2026-06-04: Source units are first-class sync targets

- Keep one `sourceConnections` table with typed provider metadata for Jira, Linear, and GitHub.
- Store syncable Jira boards, Linear Teams, and GitHub repositories in `sourceUnits`; do not cache
  available units as loose JSON on the connection.
- Team Source mappings point to `sourceUnits`, which gives sync/deep-link code stable provider
  metadata while preserving one mapping per source unit per workspace.
- Specs may point to the source unit that produced them. `sourceCycle` is sprint/cycle only;
  epic/initiative/milestone/project-style grouping is represented through structured source labels.

## 2026-06-04: RLS policies live in Drizzle schema

- Workspace-owned tables carry a direct `workspaceId` tenant key and attach a Drizzle
  `pgPolicy` generated by `workspaceIsolationPolicy(...)`; the `workspaces` root table compares
  `workspaces.id` to the same workspace session setting.
- API code must validate membership before opening a workspace-scoped transaction, then set
  `app.workspace_id` for that transaction. RLS is the database backstop; services still own
  product-specific permission checks such as workspace admin vs team admin.
- `team_members` stores `workspaceId` directly so team-role queries and RLS do not need a policy
  subquery through `teams`.

## 2026-06-03: Workspace roles and Team roles are separate scoped enums

Supersedes the shared `MembershipRole` enum from the first schema pass.

- Workspace Memberships have `role: member | admin`.
- Team Memberships have `role: member | admin`.
- Workspace admin and team admin are independent permissions stored on different tables; a
  person can be one, both, or neither. There is no single role ladder that mixes
  `workspace_admin` and `team_admin`.

## 2026-06-03: Team General settings are explicit columns, not generic JSON

Supersedes the `teams.settings` part of the 2026-06-02 schema entry.

- The Team Admin General UI controls the Stride team name plus six workflow/breakdown defaults:
  new synced spec destination, triage owner, missing-estimate behavior, unassigned-work behavior,
  ready-to-schedule rule, and stale-breakdown nudge.
- These live as typed columns on `teams`; the previous generic `settings` JSON placeholder is
  removed.
- Source-owned identifiers and source-to-team assignment stay in Team Source mapping. Team General
  does not control member working hours, working mode, notification timing, presence, or focus
  status.

## 2026-06-03: Workspace General settings are explicit columns, not generic JSON

Supersedes the `workspaces.settings` part of the 2026-06-02 schema entry.

- The Workspace Admin General UI controls workspace identity (name, address slug, logo) plus
  invite permission, Team admin grant permission, source request permission, unmapped source unit
  routing, cross-team move reviewer, and awaiting-approval destination.
- These live as typed columns on `workspaces`; the previous generic `settings` JSON placeholder
  is removed.
- Personal workspace preferences stay on the user or workspace membership, not on the Workspace:
  working hours, calendar opt-in, notification overrides, tracking preferences, working mode,
  presence, and focus status.
